{"id":166,"date":"2026-07-09T14:30:49","date_gmt":"2026-07-09T14:30:49","guid":{"rendered":"http:\/\/localhost:8181\/fixer-upper-hvac-triage-what-new-castle-bargain-hunters-should-budget\/"},"modified":"2026-07-14T19:43:56","modified_gmt":"2026-07-14T19:43:56","slug":"fixer-upper-hvac-triage-what-new-castle-bargain-hunters-should-budget","status":"publish","type":"page","link":"http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/fixer-upper-hvac-triage-what-new-castle-bargain-hunters-should-budget\/","title":{"rendered":"New Castle&#8217;s Mid-Century Furnaces Were Oversized From Day One"},"content":{"rendered":"\n<div class=\"wp-block-media-text alignfull has-media-on-the-right s-hero-split wp-elements-e633cf0db0182993e765fcd06ab99bbb\" style=\"grid-template-columns:auto 50%\"><div class=\"wp-block-media-text__content\">\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-element-heading s-hero-split__text wp-block-heading\">Forty-year-old furnaces need replacing<\/h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">New Castle&#039;s mid-century homes were built with oversized HVAC equipment designed for cheap fuel\u2014now aging out simultaneously.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-buttons is-content-justification-center is-layout-flex wp-container-core-buttons-is-layout-35f06ea7 wp-block-buttons-is-layout-flex\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-button\"><a class=\"wp-block-button__link wp-element-button\" href=\"\/contact\/\">Book Winter Readiness<\/a><\/div>\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div><figure class=\"wp-block-media-text__media\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1024\" height=\"683\" src=\"\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/7029986093_4600b63031_b.jpg\" alt=\"furnace basement heating\" class=\"wp-image-178\" style=\"object-fit:cover;height:100%\" srcset=\"http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/7029986093_4600b63031_b.jpg 1024w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/7029986093_4600b63031_b-300x200.jpg 300w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/7029986093_4600b63031_b-768x512.jpg 768w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px\" \/><span class=\"s-img-badge s-img-badge--stock\" title=\"&quot;Basement&quot; by Martin Cathrae, BY-SA, via Openverse\">Stock<\/span><\/figure><\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"s-trust-bar\"><div class=\"s-trust__item\">15+ Years Serving PA<\/div><div class=\"s-trust__item\">Licensed &amp; Insured<\/div><div class=\"s-trust__item\">Same-Day Emergency<\/div><div class=\"s-trust__item\">Local to Your Area<\/div><\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group alignfull s-band s-band--sand has-tint-background-color has-background has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-08713647 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:6rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:6rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-633910b2 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:4.5rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:4.5rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list s-pills s-pills--ember\">\n<li>Original 1960s\u201370s equipment still running<\/li>\n\n\n<li>30\u201340% ductwork air leakage typical<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Oversized furnaces short-cycling inefficiently<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Coordinated replacement saves money vs. piecemeal<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Right-sized equipment reduces capacity 40\u201350%<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Envelope upgrades first, then mechanicals<\/li>\n\n<\/ul>\n\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-633910b2 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:4.5rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:4.5rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What We Handle for Aging Mid-Century Systems<\/h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">New Castle&#039;s ranch and split-level homes need targeted solutions that account for oversized original equipment, decades of ductwork deterioration, and the challenge of replacing multiple aging systems at once. Our approach starts with honest assessment of what&#039;s actually worth saving and what must go.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list s-icon-list\">\n<li>Manual J load calculations to right-size replacement furnaces and air conditioners\u2014not oversized like original equipment<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Ductwork sealing, redesign, and insulation to eliminate 25\u201340% conditioned air loss into attics and crawlspaces<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Coordinated heating and cooling replacement with matching systems and compatible controls<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Attic and rim-joist air sealing and insulation upgrades that reduce mechanical loads before equipment replacement<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Furnace efficiency testing and combustion safety assessment to determine whether existing equipment poses risks<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Return air balancing and zoning improvements for even comfort in long ranch layouts<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Venting and electrical infrastructure updates to support modern high-efficiency equipment<\/li>\n\n<\/ul>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-633910b2 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:4.5rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:4.5rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-media-text\" style=\"grid-template-columns:50% auto\"><figure class=\"wp-block-media-text__media\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1024\" height=\"678\" src=\"\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/6791940537_40b7d0ac00_b.jpg\" alt=\"boiler pipes heating system\" class=\"wp-image-187\" srcset=\"http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/6791940537_40b7d0ac00_b.jpg 1024w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/6791940537_40b7d0ac00_b-300x199.jpg 300w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/6791940537_40b7d0ac00_b-768x509.jpg 768w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px\" \/><span class=\"s-img-badge s-img-badge--stock\" title=\"&quot;20110719-RD-LSC-0205&quot; by USDAgov, BY, via Openverse\">Stock<\/span><\/figure><div class=\"wp-block-media-text__content\">\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Why Oversized Furnaces Still Work but Waste Energy<\/h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">A furnace rated for 120,000 BTU in a home needing 60,000 satisfies thermostat demand almost immediately, then shuts off until temperature drops far enough to call for heat again. This short cycling repeats ten times per hour instead of running steady for longer, steadier cycles where combustion efficiency peaks. The result: furnace still produces heat decades later, but at 50\u201360% efficiency instead of 80\u201385%, consuming vastly more fuel while degraded components fail one by one.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li>Short cycling degrades components faster\u2014wear is driven by cycle count, not runtime hours<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Efficiency losses from cracked heat exchangers, failed draft induction, and air leakage bypass combustion heat<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Temperature swings in the home as furnace rapidly satisfies demand then stays off until temperature drops<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Never sized based on actual load calculations\u2014original contractors used rules of thumb that assumed bigger was always better<\/li>\n\n<\/ul>\n\n<\/div><\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-633910b2 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:4.5rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:4.5rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-media-text has-media-on-the-right\" style=\"grid-template-columns:auto 50%\"><div class=\"wp-block-media-text__content\">\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Ductwork Leakage and Distribution Defects in Ranch Layouts<\/h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Ranch homes in New Castle typically route supply ducts thirty to forty feet through attics or crawlspaces to reach distant bedrooms, with return air pulled from a single central grille. Summer attic temperatures hit 140\u00b0F, degrading duct insulation and tape. Leakage rates of 25\u201340% are common\u2014conditioned air escapes into unconditioned spaces while pressure imbalances create cold rooms at duct ends and overheated zones near the furnace.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li>Long duct runs lose conditioned air to friction and leakage before reaching far rooms<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Pressure imbalances from poor return air design force cool or hot air to escape through envelope leaks<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Attic ducts experience thermal cycling that accelerates insulation degradation and mastic failure<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Aeroseal pressurized particle sealing can reduce leakage 50\u201370% without opening finished ceilings and walls<\/li>\n\n<\/ul>\n\n<\/div><figure class=\"wp-block-media-text__media\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"800\" height=\"600\" src=\"\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/Ductless_Mini_Split_Heat_Pump_Installation.jpg\" alt=\"mini split heat pump\" class=\"wp-image-202\" srcset=\"http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/Ductless_Mini_Split_Heat_Pump_Installation.jpg 800w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/Ductless_Mini_Split_Heat_Pump_Installation-300x225.jpg 300w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/Ductless_Mini_Split_Heat_Pump_Installation-768x576.jpg 768w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 800px) 100vw, 800px\" \/><span class=\"s-img-badge s-img-badge--stock\" title=\"&quot;Ductless Mini Split Heat Pump Installation&quot; by Vernon Air Con, BY-SA, via Openverse\">Stock<\/span><\/figure><\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group alignfull s-band s-band--cream has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-08713647 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:6rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:6rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-633910b2 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:4.5rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:4.5rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-element-heading has-text-align-center wp-block-heading\">Replacement Planning Strategies<\/h2>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-columns alignwide s-cards-2 is-layout-flex wp-container-core-columns-is-layout-794e3cfa wp-block-columns-is-layout-flex\">\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-column s-card s-card--accent is-layout-flow wp-block-column-is-layout-flow\">\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Furnace + Air Conditioner Together<\/h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Matched systems from one manufacturer, coordinated venting, single access project. Avoid incompatibility and wasted contractor mobilization.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-column s-card s-card--accent is-layout-flow wp-block-column-is-layout-flow\">\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Envelope First, Then Mechanicals<\/h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Upgrade insulation, air seal, replace windows. Right-size replacement equipment for reduced loads\u2014often 40\u201350% less capacity needed.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-column s-card s-card--accent is-layout-flow wp-block-column-is-layout-flow\">\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Load-Calculated Sizing<\/h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Manual J calculations based on current building condition\u2014never match old equipment capacity. Right-sized furnaces run longer, steadier cycles.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-column s-card s-card--accent is-layout-flow wp-block-column-is-layout-flow\">\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Integrated Ductwork Design<\/h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Redesign supply and return systems simultaneously. Add return air grilles, shorten runs, balance pressure. Retrofit during envelope renovation if possible.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-633910b2 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:4.5rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:4.5rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Coordinated Replacement Sequence<\/h2>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list s-steps\">\n<li>1 \u2014 Whole-Home Energy Assessment \u2014 Load calculations, efficiency testing of existing equipment, ductwork blower-door audit to measure leakage, envelope thermal imaging.<\/li>\n\n\n<li>2 \u2014 Prioritize System Replacements \u2014 Age and safety risk first\u2014oldest furnace, water heater, or AC goes first. Plan for simultaneous heating\/cooling replacement if both near end-of-life.<\/li>\n\n\n<li>3 \u2014 Plan Envelope Upgrades \u2014 Target attic insulation, rim-joist sealing, air-sealing major leakage paths. Complete before HVAC replacement so new equipment is sized for improved building.<\/li>\n\n\n<li>4 \u2014 Select Right-Sized Equipment \u2014 Match furnace and air conditioner capacity to recalculated loads. Specify compatible blowers, venting, and controls for integrated operation.<\/li>\n\n\n<li>5 \u2014 Redesign Ductwork and Balance \u2014 Shorten runs, add return air capacity, insulate attic ducts, seal all connections. Test static pressure and airflow to verify balanced distribution.<\/li>\n\n<\/ul>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-633910b2 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:4.5rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:4.5rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">The Mid-Century Home Timeline Reality<\/h2>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group s-indent has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\">\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">A ranch or split-level built in 1972 is now fifty-plus years old. Original furnaces, air conditioners, water heaters, and electrical service were all installed then. They don&#039;t fail sequentially over decades\u2014they age out nearly simultaneously around the forty to fifty-year mark, creating a compressed replacement window where homeowners face multiple major system decisions at once.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The strategic response is coordinated assessment and sequenced replacement that integrates improvements, not reactive panic when the first system dies and the contractor sizes a replacement to match the broken unit it&#039;s replacing.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group alignfull s-band s-band--ink has-contrast-background-color has-background has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-08713647 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:6rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:6rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-633910b2 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:4.5rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:4.5rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-element-heading has-text-align-center wp-block-heading\">Equipment Age and Risk Assessment<\/h2>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group s-table s-table--striped has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\">\n<figure class=\"wp-block-table\"><table class=\"has-fixed-layout\"><thead><tr><th>Equipment Type<\/th><th>Typical Lifespan<\/th><th>If Installed 1972<\/th><th>Current Risk Level<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody><tr><td>Furnace<\/td><td>15\u201320 years<\/td><td>Now 52 years old<\/td><td>Replace immediately<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Air Conditioner<\/td><td>15\u201320 years<\/td><td>Now 52 years old<\/td><td>Replace immediately<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Water Heater<\/td><td>10\u201315 years<\/td><td>Now 52 years old<\/td><td>Replace immediately<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Electrical Service Panel<\/td><td>50\u201370 years<\/td><td>Now 52 years old<\/td><td>Inspect for upgrades needed<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Ductwork<\/td><td>30\u201340 years<\/td><td>Now 52 years old<\/td><td>Seal and insulate, redesign if needed<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Basement Rim Joist<\/td><td>Indefinite if sealed<\/td><td>Likely unsealed, leaking<\/td><td>Air seal and insulate now<\/td><\/tr><\/tbody><\/table><\/figure>\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-633910b2 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:4.5rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:4.5rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group s-quote s-quote--dramatic has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\">\n<figure class=\"wp-block-pullquote\"><blockquote><p>Waiting for each system to fail and replacing it in isolation means missing opportunities for integrated efficiency improvements, paying emergency service premiums, and potentially buying incompatible equipment that can&#039;t be integrated later.<\/p><cite>\u2014 Keystone Comfort Co.<\/cite><\/blockquote><\/figure>\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-633910b2 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:4.5rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:4.5rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group s-diagnostic has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-b267b866 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:3rem;padding-right:3rem;padding-bottom:3rem;padding-left:3rem\">\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Your Mid-Century HVAC Health Check<\/h3>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li>Furnace cycles on and off every 5\u201310 minutes even on moderate temperature days<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Bedrooms at far ends of home never reach thermostat setting in winter<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Significant temperature swings\u2014home overshoots setpoint then drops before furnace cycles back on<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Attic or crawlspace ducts have visible gaps, disconnections, or crushed flex duct sections<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Central return air grille\u2014no individual room returns, doors must stay open for comfort<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Furnace age unknown or more than 35 years old, or showing rust, soot, or corrosion around seams<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Air conditioner more than 20 years old or running at reduced capacity despite clear coils<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Utility bills rising year-over-year despite no change in thermostat settings or occupancy<\/li>\n\n<\/ul>\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group alignfull s-band s-band--dark-gradient s-band--spacious has-contrast-background-color has-background has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-08713647 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:6rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:6rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group s-cta--fullbleed has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\">\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Stop Running Forty-Year-Old Equipment into the Ground<\/h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">New Castle mid-century homes need coordinated assessment and replacement planning, not emergency fixes. We&#039;ll calculate what your home actually needs, not what the old broken system was oversized for.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-buttons is-content-justification-center is-layout-flex wp-container-core-buttons-is-layout-35f06ea7 wp-block-buttons-is-layout-flex\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-button\"><a class=\"wp-block-button__link wp-element-button\" href=\"\/contact\/\">Schedule a Whole-Home Assessment<\/a><\/div>\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-button is-style-outline is-style-outline--1\"><a class=\"wp-block-button__link wp-element-button\" href=\"tel:+1\">Call Now<\/a><\/div>\n<\/div>\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-633910b2 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:4.5rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:4.5rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-element-heading has-text-align-center wp-block-heading\">Mid-Century Home HVAC Questions for New Castle Homeowners<\/h2>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group s-accordion s-accordion--bordered has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-1583283a wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\">\n<details class=\"wp-block-details is-layout-flow wp-block-details-is-layout-flow\"><summary>My furnace is forty years old but still works. Should I replace it?<\/summary>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Furnaces from the nineteen-seventies that still operate are likely oversized and running at fifty to sixty percent efficiency due to degraded heat exchangers, failed safety controls, and air leakage bypassing combustion surfaces. Even if they produce heat, they&#039;re consuming substantially more fuel than modern equipment and may pose carbon monoxide risks from cracked heat exchangers or venting deterioration. Combustion safety testing and efficiency analysis can determine whether continued operation is safe and economical.<\/p>\n\n<\/details>\n\n\n\n<details class=\"wp-block-details is-layout-flow wp-block-details-is-layout-flow\"><summary>Should I replace my furnace and air conditioner at the same time?<\/summary>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">If both are near end-of-life, simultaneous replacement allows matched system selection, coordinated venting and electrical work, and ductwork modifications that benefit both heating and cooling. You&#039;ll avoid the inefficiency and compatibility issues that arise when a new furnace or air conditioner tries to work with an old, mismatched partner component, and you&#039;ll only pay once for contractor mobilization and access work.<\/p>\n\n<\/details>\n\n\n\n<details class=\"wp-block-details is-layout-flow wp-block-details-is-layout-flow\"><summary>How much smaller can my replacement furnace be compared to my existing one?<\/summary>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Proper Manual J load calculation often shows that mid-century homes with upgraded windows and added insulation need forty to fifty percent less capacity than original oversized equipment. Never size replacement equipment based on existing capacity\u2014size it based on current building conditions with proper load calculations, and you&#039;ll get better comfort, efficiency, and equipment longevity from right-sized equipment that runs longer cycles.<\/p>\n\n<\/details>\n\n\n\n<details class=\"wp-block-details is-layout-flow wp-block-details-is-layout-flow\"><summary>Is aeroseal ductwork sealing worth it if I&#039;m replacing the furnace anyway?<\/summary>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Yes\u2014whether the furnace is new or old, it&#039;s wasting conditioned air through duct leakage. Aeroseal can reduce leakage by fifty to seventy percent without opening finished ceilings and walls, and the benefit compounds over the life of your new equipment. Right-sized furnaces also benefit most from tight ductwork because the efficiency gain is largest when less air is leaking away before reaching rooms.<\/p>\n\n<\/details>\n\n\n\n<details class=\"wp-block-details is-layout-flow wp-block-details-is-layout-flow\"><summary>Should I upgrade to a heat pump instead of replacing my furnace with a new gas furnace?<\/summary>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Heat pumps make sense in mild climates or for homeowners with excellent envelope insulation who can handle a backup heat source during extreme cold. New Castle winters can run -10\u00b0F to 0\u00b0F, which is at the edge of heat pump performance. If you have a well-sealed, well-insulated home and can accept backup electric resistance heat during extended cold snaps, a heat pump offers long-term efficiency and cooling benefits.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">For existing uninsulated mid-century homes, a properly sized high-efficiency furnace is often the more practical choice\u2014but upgrade envelope first and reassess.<\/p>\n\n<\/details>\n\n\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-633910b2 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:4.5rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:4.5rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-element-heading has-text-align-center wp-block-heading\">Our Work in Your Neighborhood<\/h2>\n\n\n\n<figure class=\"wp-block-gallery alignwide has-nested-images columns-3 is-cropped s-gallery wp-block-gallery-2 is-layout-flex wp-block-gallery-is-layout-flex\">\n\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1024\" height=\"1024\" data-id=\"1756\" src=\"\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-technician-2.png\" alt=\"technician - professional HVAC service photo\" class=\"wp-image-1756\" srcset=\"http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-technician-2.png 1024w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-technician-2-300x300.png 300w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-technician-2-150x150.png 150w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-technician-2-768x768.png 768w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px\" \/><span class=\"s-img-badge s-img-badge--ai\" title=\"AI-generated (gemini-2.5-flash-image)\">AI<\/span><\/figure>\n\n\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1024\" height=\"357\" data-id=\"191\" src=\"\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/3359389452_125c1ce535_b.jpg\" alt=\"unfinished basement\" class=\"wp-image-191\" srcset=\"http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-co